Bellowsprawl

Ingredients: POST-ROCK + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Accordion  
Creative Catalyst: Its first record was cut aboard a generation ship halfway to Proxima Centauri.

A suffocating, magnificent architecture of sound where the biological wheeze of the accordion is expanded into an interstellar leviathan. Massive, slow-blooming post-rock chord progressions collide with mind-bending psychedelic tape phase and zero-gravity reed drones, climbing from fragile, mechanical gasps into titanic, ear-shattering avalanches of reverbed feedback and microtonal bellows-shredding.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 45–68 BPM. Unmetered, elastic rubato that drifts weightlessly before snapping into monolithic, lurching half-time downbeats governed by the rhythmic thud of centrifugal artificial gravity drives.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and Lydian minor tonalities colliding with microtonally detuned free-reed clusters, subterranean C-pedal drones, and blinding, prismatic suspensions that resolve only after unbearable tension.
- Production Signatures: Vacuum-Duct Reamping (routing amplified accordion and guitar tracks through depressurized ventilation corridors to capture starved acoustic decay), Rotational Tape Flanging (manually dragging tape loops across magnetized ship bulkheads to induce nauseous, cosmic pitch warble), and Bellows Gate Ducking (sidechaining towering fuzz walls directly to the physical air-intake valve of an accordion so the guitars literally gasp for breath).
- Dynamics: Glacial, continental-scale builds. Pieces begin as solitary, clacking reed whispers resembling dying respirators, slowly layering bowed guitars, modular swarms, and psychedelic delays over ten to twenty minutes before detonating into blinding, deafening cataclysms of crushing sonic pressure.

Scene & Origin Lore
In Year 73 aboard the generation vessel Arkady-7, exactly halfway between Sol and Proxima Centauri, the ship entered the "Dead Aphelion"—the psychological nadir where Earth was an invisible pinpoint and the destination was still eighty years away. To combat widespread spatial psychosis, deep-hull life-support engineers broke into the vessel's cultural archives, requisitioned four Soviet-era bayan accordions, and hooked them into industrial hydro-cooling heat pipes and the ship’s primary intercom matrix. The resulting subculture, known as The Pleaters, abandoned standardized ship uniforms for heavy canvas flight suits quilted with velvet curtains scavenged from officer lounges and wore copper acoustic resonators strapped over their sternums. Their concert rituals—dubbed "The Decompression Mass"—took place in darkened hydroponic silos, where hundreds of colonists held onto overhead safety rails in microgravity, weeping collectively as the band pushed modified bellows to near-combustion point, flooding the chamber with hallucinatory, droning walls of sound that simulated the lost atmosphere of an open sky.

Typical Instruments: Modded 120-bass Russian Bayan (fitted with magnetic pickup arrays and copper pneumatic hoses), Bowed Tenor Guitars, Baritone Bass VI, Tape-Echo Scrubbers, Life-Support Modular Rigs (synths patched to read fluctuations in ship cabin pressure), Centrifugal Spring Tanks.  
For Fans Of: Godspeed You! Black Emperor, Pauline Oliveros, Swans, Popol Vuh, Stars of the Lid.  

Fictional Band Name: The Coriolis Pleat Ensemble  
Band Description: Formed in the industrial lower decks of Arkady-7, the four-piece ensemble consists of veteran pipe fitter turned bellows-shredder Valeriya Rostov, tape-manipulator and hydroponics botanist Kaito Chen, bowed-guitar drone architect Sister Mara, and pressure-gauge percussionist Dax. They perform in complete blackout conditions, illuminated only by incandescent diagnostic bulbs clipped to their wrists. Their instruments are heavily modified relics: Rostov plays a bayan accordion wrapped in industrial thermal tape whose reeds have been hand-filed to produce unsettling microtonal dissonances that resonate specifically with the metallic resonant frequencies of the ship’s titanium spine.  
