Mood: Claustrophobic awe, oceanic dread, hallucinatory homesickness, seismic release.

Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Wax Seals Dissolved in the Cryo-Vat
- Story & Context: When a ruptured coolant line flooded Deck 9 of Arkady-7, a container of handwritten letters dispatched from Earth just before launch was swept into the sub-zero glycol tanks, turning centuries of familial farewells into pale blue sludge. Engineer Valeriya Rostov salvaged the sodden pulp and wedged it directly into her accordion’s treble reed block, discovering that the air escaping past the frozen ink produced an unearthly, weeping glissando that paralyzed the salvage crew in place. The track was recorded live in that dripping, ammonia-stinking corridor as the emergency sirens fell into sync with Dax's bowed metal percussion, capturing the moment an entire generation realized they would never read their ancestors' final thoughts. "We read the dead world through salt and steam," Rostov sang through a carbon filter, inaugurating the genre’s first documented recording.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro artistic shot of an antique parchment envelope dissolving into frozen cyan coolant fluid, its red wax seal cracking into floating fragments inside a dark industrial metal vat, surrounded by glowing amber warning lamps and frost-covered copper pipes.

Track 2: "Is That Mother's Handwriting in the Dust?"
- Story & Context: Archivist Jesse Cruz spent thirty years sequestered in the hull's signal room, secretly hoarding incoming terrestrial radio-telegrams until interstellar decay reduced the messages to undecipherable bursts of radio clicks. In Sector B's Hydro-Atrium, under the suffocating smell of damp loam and ozone, Cruz suffered a breakdown and began tracing the half-decoded transcripts into the fungal dust coating the ventilation fans. The band gathered around his workstation, translating the erratic lines of dust into graphic notation while Sister Mara drew an e-bow across her baritone strings to mimic the dying modulation of the distant solar transmitters. "I kept the silence locked beneath my ribs," Cruz admitted into the bayan’s internal pickup, confessing to the entire ship that the homeworld had gone permanently quiet four decades prior.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary astronaut's gloved hand tracing illegible cursive letters into thick white dust on a blackened steel bulkhead, backlit by the surreal green bioluminescence of hydroponic algae tanks, atmosphere thick with floating spores.

Track 3: Postmark Drift in Zero-Phase
- Story & Context: Decades after communication with Earth had ended, a rogue deep-space emergency buoy struck the observation dome with a dull, hollow clank, carrying a single unreadable capsule whose exterior postmark had been completely burned away by cosmic rays. The crew refused to open it; instead, Rostov pressed the bayan’s velvet bellows against the dome's reinforced quartz window, letting the vibration of the exterior vacuum bleed through the wood and ivory. For forty uninterrupted minutes, the ensemble built a gargantuan wall of microtonal accordion fuzz that inverted the phase of the ship's own life-support engines, turning the unread capsule into an immortal monument to unreceived love. As the final crescendo shattered an overhead monitor into a shower of harmless plastic sparks, the entire room whispered the refrain together: "No home behind us, no name ahead."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A battered interstellar postal capsule floating against the curved glass of a spaceship observation cupola, its metal skin scorched silver by cosmic rays, framed by a massive accordion silhouetted against deep space.

